## 6. End-of-Limb Force/Torque Sensor Integration

Each foot has one **6-DOF force/torque sensor** that measures ground reaction forces and moments.

**Mounting and integration:**
- Sensor is mounted directly to the distal termination bulkhead of the lower-leg module
- Located immediately proximal to the foot/end-effector, distal to both ankle pitch and ankle roll
- Form factor: {{ sensing.end_of_limb_sensing.ft_sensor.form_factor.shape }}, {{ sensing.end_of_limb_sensing.ft_sensor.form_factor.diameter_mm }}mm diameter × {{ sensing.end_of_limb_sensing.ft_sensor.form_factor.height_mm }}mm height (defined in `design/sensors.yaml`)
- Mechanical constraint: treated as hard structural envelope

**Load path requirements:**
- Ground reaction forces must pass axially through the sensor
- Structural design must avoid imposing parasitic bending moments on sensor body
- Sensor sits between distal bulkhead and foot in the structural stack

**Cost accounting:**
F/T sensors are mechanically required but **excluded from cost accounting**.

---

## 6a. Inertial Sensor Integration

{% if sensing.inertial_sensing.imu.present -%}
A single **{{ sensing.inertial_sensing.imu.axes }}-axis IMU** (gyroscope + accelerometer{{ " + magnetometer" if sensing.inertial_sensing.imu.magnetometer else "" }}) is mounted at the pelvis.

**Mounting and integration:**
- Location: {{ sensing.inertial_sensing.imu.mounting_location }}
- Co-locating the IMU with the base frame origin means its measurements need no transform before use — readings are already expressed about the frame the kinematics are defined in
- Should be mounted on the pelvis structure rather than on a motor bracket, to keep brushed-motor vibration out of the accelerometer signal
- Rigid mounting is required: any compliance between the IMU and the pelvis structure appears directly as orientation error

**Function:**
Provides pelvis orientation and angular rate. Together with joint encoders and the
foot force/torque sensors, this is what makes centre-of-mass position and velocity
observable — neither is recoverable from encoders and foot sensing alone, and both are
required feedback states for the balance methods this design targets.

**Cost accounting:**
The IMU is **included in cost accounting**, unlike the F/T sensors.
{%- else -%}
No inertial sensing is present in the MVS.
{%- endif %}

